{%- comment -%}
  The reassurances that belong next to the Checkout button.

  Usage:
    {% render 'checkout-reassurance', uid: 'cart' %}
    {% render 'checkout-reassurance', uid: 'drawer', compact: true %}

  ── Why they are here and not in the checkout ────────────────────────────

  The guide asks for four things at the payment step: an order summary with
  thumbnails and a full cost breakdown, a delivery date beside each shipping
  option, security reassurance near the payment fields, and a returns link
  and support contact.

  This store is on Shopify Basic, where the checkout is not themeable at all
  — no checkout.liquid, no checkout UI extensions, no branding API. Shopify's
  own checkout already does the order summary and the security message, and
  it links the policies in its footer. What it will not do on this plan is
  carry a support contact, and what it cannot know is an arrival date.

  So the cart carries them, because the cart is the last surface this theme
  owns. Someone who leaves at checkout because they could not find the
  returns terms, or could not see when the order lands, never had the answer
  in front of them — and the cart is the last place we can put it.

  ── What each line is ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  · The arrival date, from the same snippet and the same settings the
    product page uses, so the promise does not change between the two.
  · The returns window, linked to the real policy rather than described, and
    saying what it covers — a bare "2-day returns" reads as change-of-mind,
    which this store does not offer.
  · WhatsApp and phone, because an unanswered question at the payment step
    is an abandoned cart, and this is the plan's only chance to answer it.
  · The secure-payment line, which repeats what Shopify's checkout says, on
    the page where the decision to go there is actually made.

  Params
    uid      suffix for the delivery picker's input id, so the cart page and
             the drawer can both render this without colliding.
    compact  drops the arrival row — the drawer already carries a
             free-delivery bar and has less room to spend.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign cr_uid = uid | default: 'cart'
  assign cr_wa = settings.whatsapp_number | strip
  assign cr_days = settings.returns_window_days | default: '2'
  assign cr_returns_url = shop.refund_policy.url | default: '/pages/return-policy'
-%}

<div class="co-assure">
  {%- unless compact -%}
    <div class="co-assure-row co-assure-eta">
      {% render 'delivery-estimate', uid: cr_uid %}
    </div>
  {%- endunless -%}

  <div class="co-assure-row">
    <span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">lock</span>
    <span>Secure checkout — card details go straight to Shopify, never to us.</span>
  </div>

  <div class="co-assure-row">
    <span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">autorenew</span>
    {%- comment -%}
      Linked, and specific about what it covers. "2-day returns" on its own
      is the sentence a shopper reads as a change-of-mind window, which is
      the one thing this policy does not give them.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <span><a href="{{ cr_returns_url }}">{{ cr_days }}-day returns</a> on damaged, wrong or incomplete orders.</span>
  </div>

  {%- if cr_wa != blank -%}
    <div class="co-assure-row">
      <span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">chat</span>
      {%- comment -%}
        A real link out, not a widget. At this point the shopper has a
        specific question about their own order and wants a person — the
        float in the corner opens the same conversation, but nothing on the
        cart said it was there.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <span>Question before you pay?
        <a href="https://wa.me/{{ cr_wa }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WhatsApp us</a>
        — daily 10am–7pm.</span>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>
